  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "DB melts",
      "title": "Every page hits the database",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Your shop is popular. Every product page asks the database for the same kind of thing — name, price, photos — and it’s now 92% busy.\n\nReads queue up and pages slow to nearly half a second at p95.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Databases are usually the hardest part to scale, because they hold the data. Protect them from unnecessary work."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "450 ms",
        "hitRate": "0%",
        "dbLoad": "92%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Same reads",
      "title": "The same few things, over and over",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "Look at what’s actually being read: a small set of popular products accounts for most traffic. The database recomputes the same answers thousands of times a minute.\n\nAnswers that are read often and change rarely are perfect to keep somewhere closer and faster.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Most real workloads are skewed like this — a few hot items and a long tail of rarely-read ones."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "450 ms",
        "hitRate": "0%",
        "dbLoad": "92%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Add cache",
      "title": "Put a cache in front",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Add an in-memory [[cache]] (such as Redis or Memcached). The server checks it first: a **hit** returns in about a millisecond.\n\nOn a **miss** (amber) the server reads the database, then saves a copy in the cache for next time. This is the [[cache-aside]] pattern.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "A cache lookup is one key fetched from memory: no query planning, no joins, no disk. It typically answers 10–100× faster than the query it replaces."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Cache",
        "~ 80% of reads served from memory",
        "~ Database CPU 92% → 25%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "40 ms",
        "hitRate": "80%",
        "dbLoad": "25%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Hit rate",
      "title": "Hit rate is everything",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "The share of reads answered by the cache is the [[hit rate]]. Because misses are so much slower, small changes in hit rate matter a lot.\n\nGoing from 80% to 95% hits more than halves the average read time and cuts database load by three-quarters.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Raise hit rate by caching the right things, giving the cache enough memory, and keeping entries long enough to be reused."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Hit rate 80% → 95%",
        "~ Database CPU 25% → 8%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "25 ms",
        "hitRate": "95%",
        "dbLoad": "8%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "Stale",
      "title": "The price changed. The cache didn’t.",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "An admin updates a price from $20 to $25. The database has the new price — but the cache still holds the old copy, and that’s what users see.\n\nA cache is a second copy of the truth. Keeping copies in sync is the hard part of caching.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "“There are only two hard things in computer science: cache invalidation and naming things.” — Phil Karlton"
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Database: price $20 → $25",
        "- Cache still says $20"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "25 ms",
        "hitRate": "95%",
        "dbLoad": "8%",
        "fresh": "stale"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "db-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "cache": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sc": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Invalidate",
      "title": "Invalidate on write, expire with a TTL",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "When data changes, the server writes the database *and* deletes the cached copy (red rings). The next read misses and fetches the fresh value.\n\nAs a safety net, every entry also gets a [[TTL]] (time to live): even if an invalidation is missed, stale data disappears within a minute.\n\nWhy delete rather than overwrite the cached copy? Two writes racing each other can leave the *older* value in the cache. Deleting is always safe: the next read reloads the truth.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Pick the TTL by asking: how stale is acceptable? Seconds for prices and stock, hours for a product description."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Writes delete the cached copy",
        "+ TTL: 60 s",
        "~ Users see $25"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "26 ms",
        "hitRate": "94%",
        "dbLoad": "9%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "db-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "cache": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sc": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "Eviction",
      "title": "Memory runs out",
      "chapter": "tradeoff",
      "body": "The cache can’t hold everything — memory is expensive. When it’s full, something has to go to make room.\n\nThe usual rule is [[LRU]]: evict the entry that was *least recently used*. Hot items stay; the long tail cycles through.\n\nThe main alternative, [[LFU]], evicts the least *frequently* used entry instead — better when a burst of one-off reads would otherwise push out the real favourites.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "A cache only needs to hold the hot set. 10% of the data can easily serve 90% of reads."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Cache full",
        "~ Least recently used entries evicted",
        "~ Hit rate 94% → 92%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "30 ms",
        "hitRate": "92%",
        "dbLoad": "12%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "db-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "cache": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sc": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Stampede",
      "title": "Case: the cache stampede",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "The home page is the hottest key in the cache — 2,000 reads a second. Its TTL expires. In the next instant, every one of those requests misses and goes to the database for the same answer.\n\nThe database, used to 8% load, gets hit by a wall of identical queries and stalls. This is a [[cache stampede]] (or thundering herd).",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "The more popular a key, the more dangerous its expiry."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Hot key “home page” expires",
        "+ 2,000 simultaneous misses",
        "~ Database CPU 8% → 100%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "1.4 s",
        "hitRate": "40%",
        "dbLoad": "100%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "db-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "cache": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "us-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sc": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "short": "One fetch",
      "title": "Let one request refill it",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Fix it with [[request coalescing]]: the first miss takes a short lock and fetches from the database; everyone else waits a few milliseconds for that one result.\n\nAdd two more habits: refresh hot keys a little *before* they expire, and add random jitter to TTLs so many keys don’t expire at the same moment.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Libraries call this “single-flight”. CDNs do the same at the edge (“request collapsing”)."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ One fetch per expired key",
        "+ Early refresh + TTL jitter",
        "~ Database CPU 100% → 9%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "30 ms",
        "hitRate": "97%",
        "dbLoad": "9%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "us-4": {
          "effect": "comet"
        },
        "users-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "db-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "cache": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "us-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sc": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "short": "Missing keys",
      "title": "Case: asking for what isn’t there",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "A crawler — or an attacker — requests product IDs that don’t exist: `/product/99900017`, `…18`, `…19`. Each one misses the cache, goes to the database, finds nothing, and so leaves **nothing to cache**.\n\nEvery such request is a guaranteed miss, however often it repeats. The cache gives no protection at all, and the database is busy searching for rows that were never there. This is [[cache penetration]].",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Cache-aside only remembers answers. “No such row” is an answer too — if you forget to store it, you pay for it every time."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Requests for IDs that don’t exist",
        "~ Half of all reads now miss",
        "~ Database CPU 9% → 85%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "520 ms",
        "hitRate": "50%",
        "dbLoad": "85%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "db-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "db-8": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "cache": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "us-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd-8": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sc": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s11",
      "short": "Bloom filter",
      "title": "Remember the “no” as well",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "First fix: [[negative caching]]. Store “not found” in the cache too, with a short TTL, so a repeated request for the same missing ID stops at the cache.\n\nBut a crawler never repeats itself. For that, keep a [[Bloom filter]] of every real ID in the server’s memory: a compact bit set that answers “definitely not here” or “probably here” in microseconds. Impossible IDs get a 404 without touching the cache or the database.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "A Bloom filter never says “no” to a key that exists; it occasionally says “maybe” to one that doesn’t. A 1% false-positive rate costs only about 10 bits per key."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Cache “not found” · TTL 30 s",
        "+ Bloom filter of real IDs on the server",
        "~ Database CPU 85% → 9%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "28 ms",
        "hitRate": "95%",
        "dbLoad": "9%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "db-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "db-8": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "cache": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "us-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd-8": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "sc": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s12",
      "short": "Layers",
      "title": "Caches everywhere",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "The cache we built is one layer of many. A request may be answered by the **browser** cache, the **CDN**, an **in-process** cache on the server, a shared cache like Redis, or the database’s own memory.\n\nThe closer to the user, the faster and cheaper the hit — and the harder it is to invalidate.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Most of “making it fast” is deciding what can be cached, where, and for how long."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "25 ms",
        "hitRate": "95%",
        "dbLoad": "8%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-3": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "db-3": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "cache": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "us-3": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "sd-3": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "sc": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s13",
      "short": "Recap",
      "title": "Recap",
      "chapter": "recap",
      "body": "• Cache answers that are **read often and change rarely**.\n• **Cache-aside**: check the cache, fall back to the database, then fill the cache.\n• **Hit rate** drives both speed and database load.\n• Plan for staleness: **invalidate on write**, add a **TTL**, evict with **LRU**.\n• Protect hot keys from **stampedes**: one fetch, early refresh, jittered TTLs.\n• Cache **“not found”** too, and screen impossible keys with a **Bloom filter**.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Next up: **Content Delivery** — caching files on servers spread around the world, close to your users."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "p95": "25 ms",
        "hitRate": "95%",
        "dbLoad": "8%",
        "fresh": "fresh"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    }
  ]
